---
/**
 * Regional map   the illustrated aerial photograph, full bleed.
 *
 * STATUS: raster-only, labels baked into pixels.
 *
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WHY IT IS FULL BLEED NOW
 *
 * It was a 2fr column beside a 1fr text column inside the 1280 container: at
 * 1440 that put a drawing whose labels are baked in at a native 1280 px on
 * screen at roughly 760. `Králický Sněžník 1423 m n. m.` came out around 9 px
 * tall, so the section was a picture OF a map rather than a map, and the reader
 * got the same information from it at 1440 as they did at 390   which is none.
 *
 * docs/02-design-system.md §3 already licenses this: "photography escapes the
 * container; text never does". So the heading and lead keep their measure inside
 * `.wrap` and the plate runs edge to edge under them, at up to 90rem   near
 * enough the drawing's own 1280 that it is never upscaled by more than an eighth
 * and every label is legible without leaning in.
 *
 * The section is also flatter for it, deliberately. What comes before it is the
 * three-layer parallax stage and what comes after is a dense catalogue; this one
 * is meant to be a single wide plate and a caption, the quiet beat between them.
 * Do not put a second column back beside it.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * ⚠ DISTANCE CONFLICTS IN THIS IMAGE
 * The raster shows two distances that contradict the verified table in DOSTUPNOST:
 *
 *   - Králíky: map shows 7 km, distance table has 3 km (and that table is the
 *     internally consistent source   docs/01-content.md §5.2 header note).
 *   - Králický Sněžník trail: map shows 17 km / 4 h 15 min, the old
 *     /tipy-na-vylety/ copy said 14 km, the data keeps null pending client
 *     confirmation (docs/01-content.md §9 item 18).
 *
 * The alt text below names the places without repeating the contradicted figures.
 * Do NOT add chips or labels next to this image that name either distance   it
 * would put a different number alongside the one baked into the pixels. Making
 * the plate bigger makes this MORE important, not less: at 1440 the `7 km / 9 min`
 * beside Králíky is now perfectly readable.
 *
 * TODO(client)   §9 item 18: confirm the Sněžník trail distance (14 km or 17 km)
 *   so the entry in VYLETY can be given a vzdalenost instead of null.
 * TODO(client)   §7.4 / §4: supply a clean aerial photograph with no labels
 *   baked in, so the map can be rebuilt as photograph + inline SVG <text>. That
 *   is still the real fix   full bleed buys legibility, not searchable text.
 *
 * Band: --kamen. Follows the photographic opener and precedes --omitka.
 */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import mapa from '@img/situace1-1.webp';

/* U+00A0   non-breaking space. */
const NBSP = ' ';
---

<section class="mapa" aria-labelledby="mapa-nadpis">
  <div class="wrap mapa-header" data-reveal>
    <h2 id="mapa-nadpis">Poloha chalupy a okolí</h2>
    <p class="mapa-lead">
      Mapa ukazuje dosažitelné cíle od chalupy. Turistické stezky se dají chytit
      přímo u dveří{NBSP}  jedna vede až na vrchol Králického Sněžníku.
    </p>
  </div>

  <figure class="mapa-figure" data-reveal>
    {/*
      ---- WHY THE PLATE STILL PANS ON A PHONE ----
      Full bleed fixes the laptop and does nothing for a 390 px viewport, where
      even edge to edge is under a third of the drawing's native width. Below
      48rem the figure is a horizontal scroller and the plate holds a 40rem
      minimum, so the reader pans it at a size the baked-in type can be read at.
      From 48rem up the container is wider than that minimum, `overflow-x: auto`
      never has anything to scroll, and this is an ordinary block.

      `tabindex="0"` is REQUIRED, not decoration: a scrollable region that cannot
      be focused cannot be scrolled from a keyboard, which is WCAG 2.1.1.
      `role="group"` plus the label is what stops a screen reader announcing it
      as an unnamed scroll region.
    */}
    <div
      class="mapa-pan"
      tabindex="0"
      role="group"
      aria-label="Ilustrovaná mapa okolí   na malé obrazovce ji lze posouvat do stran"
    >
      <div class="mapa-plate">
        <Image
          src={mapa}
          alt="Ilustrovaný letecký snímek okolí chalupy Heřmanka v Heřmanicích u Králík s vyznačenými destinacemi: Polsko a Międzygórze na severu se sjezdovkami 30–60 minut, hora Klepáč 1145 m n. m. jako rozvodí do trojice evropských moří, Králický Sněžník 1423 m n. m. nejvyšší hora Čech s pramenem řeky Moravy, lyžařské středisko Dolní Morava se Stezkou v oblacích a Sky Bridge 721, barokní klášter Hedeč mariánské poutní místo a město Králíky"
          widths={[640, 960, 1280]}
          sizes="(min-width: 64rem) 1280px, (min-width: 48rem) 100vw, 40rem"
          width={1280}
          height={958}
          format="webp"
          quality={72}
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    {/*
      The one caption this figure gets, and it is an instruction rather than a
      description   the heading and lead above already say what the map is and
      the <img> alt says it to a screen reader. `aria-hidden` because the panning
      affordance is already in the region's own label, and it is hidden outright
      from 48rem where there is nothing to pan.
    */}
    <figcaption class="mapa-hint" aria-hidden="true">Mapu posuňte prstem do stran.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<style>
  .mapa {
    background-color: var(--color-kamen);
    /* Not `.section`: the plate needs less air under it than over it, or the
       band reads as a picture floating in a wide grey field. */
    padding-block: 4rem 3rem;
  }

  @media (width >= 48rem) {
    .mapa {
      padding-block: 6rem 4rem;
    }
  }

  @media (width >= 64rem) {
    .mapa {
      padding-block: 8rem 5rem;
    }
  }

  h2 {
    /* --mosaz-tmava on --kamen = 5.06:1 */
    color: var(--color-mosaz-tmava);
  }

  /*
   * ⚠ NO `max-width` ON `.mapa-header`. It carries the `.wrap` class, and `.wrap`
   * is `max-width: 80rem; margin-inline: auto`   so narrowing it here narrows the
   * CONTAINER, and the auto margins then centre the heading and lead in the
   * middle of the page. This section shipped like that for one build. The measure
   * belongs on the children, where it constrains the type without touching the
   * container that positions it. §3 puts this site left of centre with the right
   * third open; the plate below is the only thing here that goes full width.
   */
  .mapa-header h2 {
    max-width: 22ch;
  }

  .mapa-lead {
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-size: var(--text-lead);
    max-width: 46ch;
    /* --kura on --kamen = 9.96:1 */
    color: var(--color-kura);
  }

  /* ---- The plate --------------------------------------------------------- */

  .mapa-figure {
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0;
    /*
     * ⚠ `min-width: 0` is what stops this section putting a horizontal scrollbar
     * on the PAGE. The plate inside carries `min-width: 40rem` for the phone pan,
     * and a block's automatic minimum size would otherwise force the whole
     * section to 40rem on a 390 px screen. The pan container is the only thing on
     * this page allowed to scroll sideways.
     */
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /*
   * ⚠ Nothing carrying `data-drift` may ever go inside this. `overflow-x: auto`
   * makes it a scroll container, and `view()` resolves against the nearest
   * ancestor scroll container   a drifting layer in here would take its timeline
   * from a box that scrolls sideways and would sit frozen. See `[data-drift]` in
   * global.css.
   */
  .mapa-pan {
    overflow-x: auto;
    overscroll-behavior-x: contain;
  }

  .mapa-plate {
    min-width: 40rem;
    background-color: var(--color-les);
  }

  @media (width >= 48rem) {
    .mapa-plate {
      min-width: 0;
      /*
       * ---- AS WIDE AS IT CAN BE WITHOUT GETTING TALLER THAN THE SCREEN ----
       *
       * A true 100vw bleed at 1440 makes this plate 1078 px tall, which is more
       * than a laptop viewport   so the reader can never see the whole map at
       * once, which is the one thing a map has to allow. The first pass shipped
       * that and it was worse than the small version it replaced: bigger type,
       * and you still could not take it in.
       *
       * So the width is driven by the HEIGHT budget. The drawing is 1280 �  958,
       * a ratio of 1.336; 88svh of height at that ratio is the widest the plate
       * can be and still fit under the fixed header with room to spare. The
       * 90rem ceiling is the second limit: the raster is 1280 px native, and
       * past about 1440 the browser is upscaling the small baked-in type that is
       * the whole value of the image.
       *
       * At 1440 �  900 that resolves to ~1058 px, at 1920 �  1080 to the native
       * 1280. Both are far past the ~760 px this used to render at inside a
       * two-column grid, and both fit on the screen.
       *
       * ⚠ The 1.336 is the image's aspect ratio. Change the plate and change it.
       */
      max-width: min(90rem, calc(88svh * 1.336));
      margin-inline: auto;
    }
  }

  .mapa-plate :global(img) {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  .mapa-hint {
    margin-top: 0.625rem;
    padding-inline: 1.5rem;
    font-size: var(--text-caption);
    /* --kura on --kamen = 9.96:1 */
    color: var(--color-kura);
  }

  @media (width >= 48rem) {
    .mapa-hint {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>
